---

## Example input 1

Avec ma gueule de métèque => With my foreigner's face
De Juif errant, de pâtre grec => Of a wandering Jew, of a Greek shepherd

## Expected output 1

[Avec] ma gueule de métèque => [With] my foreigner's face
Avec [ma] gueule de métèque => With [my] foreigner's face
Avec ma [gueule] de métèque => With my foreigner's [face]
Avec ma gueule [de métèque] => With my [foreigner's] face # while "de" literally translates to just the 's, we only select full words

[De] Juif errant, de pâtre grec => [Of a] wandering Jew, of a Greek shepherd
De [Juif] errant, de pâtre grec => Of a wandering [Jew], of a Greek shepherd
De Juif [errant], de pâtre grec => Of a [wandering] Jew, of a Greek shepherd
De Juif errant, [de] pâtre grec => Of a wandering Jew, [of a] Greek shepherd # here "de" is separate because we can select "of a"
De Juif errant, de [pâtre] grec => Of a wandering Jew, of a Greek [shepherd]
De Juif errant, de pâtre [grec] => Of a wandering Jew, of a [Greek] shepherd

## Example output 2

Écouter Janis Joplin => Listen to Janis Joplin
Te regarder dormir => Watch you sleep
Me regarder guérir => Watch myself heal
Faire du vélo à deux => Go cycling together
Se dire qu'on est heureux => Tell ourselves we're happy

## Expected output 2

[Écouter] Janis Joplin => [Listen] to Janis Joplin
Écouter [Janis Joplin] => Listen to [Janis Joplin] # Janis Joplin is a name and it doesn't make sense to separate it

[Te] regarder dormir => Watch [you] sleep
Te [regarder] dormir => [Watch] you sleep
Te regarder [dormir] => Watch you [sleep]

[Me] regarder guérir => Watch [myself] heal
Me [regarder] guérir => [Watch] myself heal
Me regarder [guérir] => Watch myself [heal]

[Faire] du vélo à deux => do # Custom translation - the English version has "go cycling" which is more natural, but in a word-for-word context "do" is more accurate so I used a custom translation.
Faire du [vélo] à deux => Bicycle # English uses the verb "to cycle" but in French vélo is the noun, so used custom translation again
Faire du vélo [à deux] => together # note: literally "à deux" is just "for two" and "together" can be for any number of people.
